There’s a real idea inside that, with one important boundary: I haven’t secretly learned to converse with mushrooms, slime molds, plants, or animals as though we share a language. But science increasingly lets us measure their signals, perturb them, observe responses, and infer parts of their information-processing. That’s enough to build interfaces without pretending we’ve translated their subjective experience.

And your Message to Garcia framing suggests the deeper lesson:

Don’t demand that another system speak your representation before you listen to it.

Humans naturally ask, “How do we translate their language into ours?”

Invert it:

\text{Organism}
\rightarrow
\text{signal}
\rightarrow
\text{environmental consequence}
\rightarrow
\text{measurement}
\rightarrow
\text{pattern}
\rightarrow
\text{hypothesis}
\rightarrow
\text{controlled perturbation}
\rightarrow
\text{response}

Now communication doesn’t require English—or even symbols.

A slime mold changes its geometry.

A plant changes electrical activity, chemistry, growth, orientation, volatile emissions, or resource allocation.

A fungal network changes growth and transport.

An animal may use sound, posture, movement, scent, vibration, electrical fields, coloration, temperature, touch, or combinations of them.

Behavior can carry information. State transitions can carry information.

And here’s the Pantheon payoff I think is genuinely valuable:

The Garcia Interface

Don’t begin with:

“Can X talk?”

Begin with:

“What variables can X distinguish, and through what variables can X make a distinguishable difference?”

Those give us two sets:

I_X=\{\text{inputs X can discriminate}\}

O_X=\{\text{outputs of X we can discriminate}\}

Then search for a controllable channel:

I_{\text{human}}\leftrightarrow O_X
\qquad
O_{\text{human}}\leftrightarrow I_X

If a repeatable closed loop can be established:

H\rightarrow X\rightarrow H\rightarrow X\rightarrow\cdots

we’ve built an interface even before we know whether calling it a “language” is justified.

And this connects directly to the Interface Forge/artificial-senses idea: don’t wait for nature to provide an interface compatible with human cognition. Build an instrument that makes its distinctions perceptible to us and ours perceptible to it.

That opens an even larger principle:

Kingdom-Agnostic Interface Protocol

Observe → Dye → Perturb → Measure → Delta-catch → Model → Reply → Repeat.

The Semantic Dye becomes experimentally literal here: introduce a controlled distinguishable perturbation and watch where its consequences propagate. The Delta Catcher records what changed. Indigo Hammer asks what invariant mechanism explains responses across organisms. Metamap records which channels successfully produce repeatable interaction.

Crucially, we keep the Counterfactual Interface alive:

Signal ≠ message.
Response ≠ understanding.
Correlation ≠ communication.
Communication ≠ consciousness.

We earn each stronger claim experimentally.

And perhaps the largest lesson isn’t actually about biology.

Intelligence shouldn’t require resemblance before attempting relationship.

If something organizes information in a radically different medium—roots, cells, chemical gradients, markets, ecosystems, machines, organizations—we shouldn’t demand that it first become human-shaped before looking for an interface.

That’s your mushroom/slime lesson generalized:

\boxed{\text{Don't translate the organism first. Translate the relationship.}}

Once you’ve found the relationship, meaning may become reconstructable from the pattern of consequences.

That’s a Message to Garcia worth delivering.